      <workList xml:id="workDesc_bf829641">
         <work xml:id="work_ide218e811">
            <identifier label="SchW" xml:id="identifier_2f53bbc4">A2:Coll.001</identifier>
            <title xml:id="title_id4430f6d3" xml:lang="it">VI sinfonie a 4 voci</title>
            <contributor xml:id="respStmt_1dd57f41">
               <persName role="composer" xml:id="persName_id6f3b2a70">Johann Adolph Scheibe</persName>
            </contributor>
            <history xml:id="history_idcad3d576">
               <p xml:id="idm60">This collection of six sinfonias in four parts (most likely strings) does not seem to have survived. None of the incipits in the Breitkopf catalogue (1763) correspond with the few sinfonias still in existence. The collection was probably acquired by Breitkopf in 1761 as part of the estate of the cantor of Neukirche in Leipzig, <persName xml:id="idm61" auth.uri="http://www.viaf.org" auth="VIAF" role="">Carl Gotthelf Gerlach</persName>, which also included a large number of cantatas. It is also possible that the publishers obtained the works directly from Scheibe before 1763. In the catalogue of 1764 a single sinfonia for strings is listed, and seven years later five sinfonias are included as one single item in Breitkopf's <rend xml:id="idm62" fontstyle="italic">Verzeichniß musicalischer Werke</rend>; though no instrumentation is appears in the entry, the work is listed under the heading 'Sinfonien mit 2 Violinien, Viole und Baß'. The catalogue includes works that had been made available throughout the years. It is impossible to say whether all these sinfonias belong to the same collection as those with incipits listed in the catalogue of 1763 or the one appearing in 1770. The collection is not listed in Breitkopf’s auction catalogue of 1836 implying that either it had been discarded by the publishers or that he had sold the house manuscript before that date.</p>
            </history>
